WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:10.179
So, Mr. Tully said this project could be
about any biology topic we're interested in.

00:00:10.179 --> 00:00:14.189
And it could be a research paper or illustration or
a video or something?

00:00:14.189 --> 00:00:16.119
So, class, here's my video.

00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:20.349
It's about some of you. So pay attention.

00:00:20.349 --> 00:00:21.640
I'm overweight.

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.699
And it's obviously a hard thing to deal
with for all sorts of reasons but the

00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:29.649
biggest problem I've been having
recently is not about me, personally,

00:00:29.649 --> 00:00:31.380
it's about everyone else.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.840
It's how everyone else sees me and treats me
just because I'm overweight.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.540
And all of you might assume things about
me, about all fat people, that probably

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:45.540
aren't true.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.970
So, my project is talking about these
myths about weight and prejudice

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:03.970
and getting some actual facts out there.

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.570
You know how Mr. Tully assigned this
project, like,

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.680
a month and a half ago?

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.950
Well, for the last month and a half,

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:15.530
I've been bringing this camera to school

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.610
in here, just like this.

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.600
So, it picked up pretty much everything you said,

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:27.600
so don't be surprised to hear yourself
in this video.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:41.080
Here's a little snippet from Arthur and
Mr. Tully from three weeks ago.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:54.560
As you well know will be hearing much
more from Arthur, and we'll see if he can top that.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.660
And, we'll see if Tully did anything about it like
a good and caring teacher would.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.520
And now,

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.520
I present the statistics.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.579
In the past twenty years

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:07.040
obesity in children and adults

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.200
has doubled.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:12.519
In that same time, obesity
in teenagers has tripled.

00:02:12.519 --> 00:02:15.879
Now what am I supposed to do when I hear
those numbers?

00:02:15.879 --> 00:02:17.359
I don't know.

00:02:17.359 --> 00:02:23.389
Those statistics are really scary but I
don't think about obesity like that.

00:02:23.389 --> 00:02:24.040
All I know,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:28.430
is that I have to deal with a bunch of stereotypes
and prejudices every day, all over the

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:29.370
place

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.209
and at school,

00:02:32.209 --> 00:02:33.849
I get picked on.

00:02:33.849 --> 00:02:39.739
I'm not going to pretend it isn't harmful 
because it is.

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:41.329
"Is that even legal?"

00:02:41.329 --> 00:02:44.829
"Do I need to get my lawyer?"
"Looking for Twinkies?"

00:02:44.829 --> 00:02:51.809
"Hey, uh, what's with the camera there?"

00:02:51.809 --> 00:02:56.279
Just because I'm heavy, other people
assume I'm lazy, stupid,

00:02:56.279 --> 00:02:57.319
mean,

00:02:57.319 --> 00:02:59.549
unpopular and that I eat too much.

00:02:59.549 --> 00:03:01.829
But stereotypes aren't the truth.

00:03:01.829 --> 00:03:03.499
They're mean and annoying.

00:03:03.499 --> 00:03:10.499
But most of all, they're wrong.

00:03:15.169 --> 00:03:17.399
MR. TULLY: These are the genes that are responsible for

00:03:17.399 --> 00:03:19.430
things like eye color,

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:21.309
skin color,

00:03:21.309 --> 00:03:26.029
being left-handed or right-handed.
ARTHUR: Does it affect being fat?

00:03:26.029 --> 00:03:29.749
MR. TULLY: Uh, some people people say that it's
their genes that affect their weight.

00:03:29.749 --> 00:03:31.850
ARTHUR: It's not genes, it's Twinkies.

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:33.850
[laughter]

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:39.459
LAUREN: Or pancakes. [laughter]
MEGAN: Or sitting around doing nothing. [laughter]

00:03:39.459 --> 00:03:45.089
ALEX: Or ice cream with gravy on it. [laughter]
I'm going to have to call a foul on that twinkie

00:03:45.089 --> 00:03:46.329
comment.

00:03:46.329 --> 00:03:50.049
Arthur said it but you, Mr. Tully, you've
been teaching biology for years and I thought maybe

00:03:50.049 --> 00:03:52.269
you'd have the facts.

00:03:52.269 --> 00:03:56.609
Well let's find out what the causes of
obesity are from, you know,

00:03:56.609 --> 00:04:00.260
experts? 
The causes of obesity are very complex.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:04.860
It is a combination of genetic factors,
environmental factors, psychological 

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:05.859
factors

00:04:05.859 --> 00:04:11.339
and it's important to recognize that so
that when we look at someone, and we see

00:04:11.339 --> 00:04:14.809
whatever their body weight is we don't
make assumptions about their behaviors

00:04:14.809 --> 00:04:18.869
based on that information.

00:04:18.869 --> 00:04:20.069
Whoa! That's weird!

00:04:20.069 --> 00:04:25.310
That expert researcher with the Ph.D just said that
the Twinkie thing was wrong. Let's double-check.

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:30.360
ARTHUR: It's not genes, it's Twinkies.
DR. SCHWARTZ: Genetic factors, environmental factors,

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.469
psychological factors.

00:04:32.469 --> 00:04:37.050
Right. Of course if someone just sits around and eats miles
and miles of twinkles all day they'll gain weight.

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:40.659
But I don't just sit around and I don't eat
junk food.

00:04:40.659 --> 00:04:43.229
Maybe the causes obesity are very complex.

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:50.229
The causes of obesity are very complex.

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:00.969
Are you going to keep sitting in front of that 
camera till the end of time?

00:05:00.969 --> 00:05:03.059
No, just until the tape runs out.

00:05:03.059 --> 00:05:06.739
The project is due in a week and I haven't
even added graphics.

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:07.850
Well, I haven't even done,

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:14.850
anything.

00:05:17.409 --> 00:05:19.479
MR. TULLY: So, coming back to that

00:05:19.479 --> 00:05:21.699
if we looked around this room

00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:23.040
you see lots of different

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.069
body shapes and sizes

00:05:25.069 --> 00:05:26.550
so lots of different diets.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.080
Then you may remember he singled out
two of us

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:31.529
me and my friend, Erica.

00:05:31.529 --> 00:05:33.989
MR. TULLY: So someone like Erica

00:05:33.989 --> 00:05:35.119
probably had a

00:05:35.119 --> 00:05:37.740
pretty healthy breakfast this morning.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:39.979
Someone like Bene,

00:05:39.979 --> 00:05:41.539
uh, I don't know, probably had

00:05:41.539 --> 00:05:45.360
ARTHUR: Pancakes. Again. [laughter]
BENE: No, I had toast and an apple.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.049
ARTHUR: Oh, you mean an apple pie.

00:05:48.049 --> 00:05:53.089
MR. TULLY: [laughing] What Arthur is trying to say is that

00:05:53.089 --> 00:05:54.220
having a healthy diet and staying active

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:58.200
and exercising, they're
really the only things that are keeping you from 

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:00.179
having a normal body.

00:06:00.179 --> 00:06:01.020
ARTHUR: And I'm right. Just look at 

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.919
Erica.

00:06:04.919 --> 00:06:06.110
Okay. Two things.

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:06.819
One

00:06:06.819 --> 00:06:10.439
I've been hanging with Erica since like the
second grade and she spends a whole lot

00:06:10.439 --> 00:06:13.439
of time eating junk food and watching tv.

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:16.509
And she's still, like, a size zero.

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:18.930
Myth: Erica eats nothing.

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:22.459
Fact: Erica won a pie-eating contest two years ago.

00:06:22.459 --> 00:06:25.620
Yes, fact, she did win a pie eating contest.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:29.520
Can you not? I'm trying to make a point.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.569
Okay. One, Erica has a crazy diet.

00:06:31.569 --> 00:06:32.360
Two, 

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:35.419
here is my morning routine.

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:38.439
I get up, 

00:06:38.439 --> 00:06:39.719
early.

00:06:39.719 --> 00:06:41.349
I have an apple, or 

00:06:41.349 --> 00:06:41.860
a banana.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:43.220
and some O.J.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:45.199
with toast.

00:06:45.199 --> 00:06:51.120
And then I take Brutus for a walk.
Just a leisurely stroll.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.389
Then I get ready and head to 
school and have to hear stuff like this

00:06:55.389 --> 00:06:59.660
"It's not genes."
"Pancakes." [laughter]
"Twinkies."

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:02.940
"Ice cream with gravy on it."
"Pancakes!"
"Sitting around doing nothing."

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:05.239
It's complicated.

00:07:05.239 --> 00:07:09.199
Healthy diet and exercise are important
but they aren't going to make me shrink.

00:07:09.199 --> 00:07:12.709
And I gain more- 
Diet, exercise and a shrink right.

00:07:12.709 --> 00:07:15.979
And I'd gain more weight if I eat Erica does.

00:07:15.979 --> 00:07:20.229
If I'm eating healthy and staying active then I'm
probably in better shape than Erica.

00:07:20.229 --> 00:07:23.559
Just because someone's skinny doesn't mean
anything.

00:07:23.559 --> 00:07:25.589
Look, here's another doctor.

00:07:25.589 --> 00:07:27.939
We talked to parents about

00:07:27.939 --> 00:07:31.789
healthy lifestyles. We find that one of
the biggest misconceptions they have is

00:07:31.789 --> 00:07:35.879
that equating being thin with being
healthy or being obese would be

00:07:35.879 --> 00:07:39.349
unhealthy and that's just not
necessarily the case. There's a lot more

00:07:39.349 --> 00:07:43.089
that determines obesity besides what
kinds of foods you eat, what kinds of

00:07:43.089 --> 00:07:48.639
physical activity you do or what sedentary
behaviors you do. A lot of it is familial or

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:51.479
genetic and really outside of the child's
control.

00:07:51.479 --> 00:07:56.560
And so the important thing is to always
value health over appearance.

00:07:58.759 --> 00:08:05.349
That was a tough geometry test.

00:08:05.349 --> 00:08:09.379
[laughter]

00:08:09.379 --> 00:08:10.800
ARTHUR: Hey Erica,

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.939
what'd you do, bring your pet pig to school?

00:08:12.939 --> 00:08:14.629
[shrill laughter]

00:08:14.629 --> 00:08:15.669
Arthur, why are you breathing?

00:08:15.669 --> 00:08:22.050
ARTHUR: Does your sister know you have her hoodie?
Shut up! .. Jerk.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.349
Let's clarify what's happened there. 

00:08:24.349 --> 00:08:28.060
Arthur and Megan walked by and called me a
pig.

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:35.060
A pet pig, actually.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:42.250
Arthur and others are clearly 
prejudice against overweight

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:46.630
people and there aren't any scientific
facts to back them up.

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:49.830
What if Mr. Tully said something like,

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:53.980
MR. TULLY: (Bene's voice) people with blue eyes usually have 
lower IQs than everyone else. It's true!

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:57.210
If you see someone with blue eyes you should just
assume they're dumb.

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:59.690
ARTHUR: (Bene's voice) And ugly!

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:01.060
If everyone thought that,

00:09:01.060 --> 00:09:03.970
people with blue eyes even if they were
really smart would start to feel pretty

00:09:03.970 --> 00:09:05.640
bad about themselves.

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:08.970
Hearing all the name calling and 
laughing behind their back. You know,

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:11.610
"Hey Blue-y."

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:14.440
Generally people view blue-eyed people as,

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:15.860
stupid, mean, 

00:09:15.860 --> 00:09:16.610
ugly,

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:19.410
lazy and unpopular.

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:21.570
That seems pretty ridiculous.

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:23.460
Having mean thoughts about somebody for

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:26.110
being the way they are and looking the way they
do?

00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:28.340
It's childish, it's

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:30.120
ignorant.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:33.180
JOHN: I just need it for the next few periodsи
BENE: I don't have an extra tape.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:36.780
JOHN: Cake? for lunch?
BENE: And you've been wasting it.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:41.440
JOHN: I'm not wasting it. I think I'm actually getting 
Megan's ego on camera.

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:44.510
It shows up as an evil aura.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:50.170
MEGAN: Wouldn't it be easier to smear that cake
on your thighs?
ARTHUR: You can stop carbo-loading now.
GIRL: How many lunch periods do you have?

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:53.560
Um, you're only supposed to have one, and 
I think that may be a problem.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:56.920
She's having a salad.
MEGAN: She shouldn't be having anything at all!

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:03.920
She looks like a pig.

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:13.360
BENE: Turn it off, John.
JOHN: You need this.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:17.270
This is why you're bringing it to school.

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:23.420
I'm not turning it off.
ERICA: Don't worry, they're just mean kids.
JOHN: Yeah.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:26.420
BENE: A pig?
JOHN: You're not a pig.

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:30.420
BENE: Maybe they're right. It's just.. people make

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:31.440
fun of me all the time.

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:34.570
And I just put up with it.
JOHN: You're gonna be alright..

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:35.930
It's not like this hasn't-

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:38.840
BENE: This hasn't happened before?

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:44.370
Yeah, I know, thanks.

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:51.370
JOHN: Hey, if you go to the nurse and say you 
have pink eye, you can get outta here.

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:54.860
BENE: I didn't go to the nurse and lie to her
and go home.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:56.850
I sucked it up and went to gym.

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:58.820
And guess what happened there.

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:01.100
I got teased in the locker room,

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:05.640
and when we were picking teams for soccer, 
Jen Martin called me thunder thighs.

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:07.350
I scored a goal, though, but

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:09.090
no one seemed to care.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:12.080
You know that scale in the locker room?

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:16.190
Last year we all got weighed in a big group
for some reason and Ms. Keenan skipped my name on the list

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:19.210
and weighed me after everyone
was gone.

00:11:19.210 --> 00:11:21.700
She thought she was being nice, I guess?

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:25.620
But it turned into this whole joke where
they said they couldn't use that scale

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:29.540
because he didn't go up high enough.

00:11:29.540 --> 00:11:32.110
I showed a lot of name-calling and ridicule.

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:37.090
But sometimes its when people don't say
anything that hurts the most.

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:41.070
People deliberately look away from the
me when I pass them in the hallway.

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:43.240
It's like if we make eye contact

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:48.430
or say hi to each other, I'll make them gain
a hundred pounds on the spot.

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:52.990
What I'm saying is it's not just the
teasing that's the problem.

00:11:52.990 --> 00:11:56.410
Bias and prejudice against people with
thunder thighs is something that's

00:11:56.410 --> 00:11:57.810
automatic.

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:00.210
Someone sees a fat person,

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:02.270
someone sees me,

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:04.390
and they already have me pegged.

00:12:04.390 --> 00:12:06.790
They already know everything about me

00:12:06.790 --> 00:12:08.830
and what they want to think of me.

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:12.490
And what I have to live with everyday is
knowing that people automatically think

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:15.930
less of me.

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:20.350
Weight bias has a range of negative consequences
and students who are teased or

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:21.959
victimized because of their weight

00:12:21.959 --> 00:12:25.350
are vulnerable to depression, anxiety,

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:29.620
low self-esteem and poor body image,
all of which can take a considerable

00:12:29.620 --> 00:12:34.550
toll on emotional well-being. In addition
weight bias can increase vulnerability to

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:38.080
unhealthy eating behaviors and
avoidance of physical activity both of

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:43.030
which may reinforce additional weight
gain and obesity.

00:12:43.030 --> 00:12:46.080
It seems like the prejudice does a lot of
harm.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:48.550
Do you really want to hurt someone like that?

00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:53.360
Were all those mean jokes funny enough for my 
depressiion and stress and anxiety to be

00:12:53.360 --> 00:12:55.150
worth it?

00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:56.179
It hurts.

00:12:56.179 --> 00:12:58.660
It still does.

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:03.060
Because every time I'm in biology, around Arthur
who thinks it's okay to make fun of me,

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:04.850
and around Tully who just doesn't care

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:08.890
I'm constantly reminded that people
don't respect me. Why?

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:11.040
Because I have more fat cells than them?

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:12.840
Which, by the way,

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:15.390
I don't have total control over.

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:17.910
There are biological and genetic
contributors

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:24.910
to bodyweight which people do not have
total control over.

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:36.230
I wish there was an easy solution to this
problem.

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:40.390
I wish that people weren't so
superficial and that the prejudices and

00:13:40.390 --> 00:13:44.480
biases or whatever you want to
call them just didn't exist.

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:47.510
I guess you just gotta realize that people are
different

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:50.870
and that's probably the coolest thing about people as
a whole.

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:54.040
Discriminating because of weight is
just as ignorant and mean as discriminating

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:56.430
because of skin color or gender.

00:13:56.430 --> 00:13:57.930
JOHN: The girl's right.

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:01.910
BENE: And I'm sure-
JOHN: What if Arthur made fun of Dave because he's in
a wheelchair? And Mr. Tully just let it

00:14:01.910 --> 00:14:04.280
happen all the time? It would be on the news
or something.

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:06.260
BENE: Yeah!

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:09.580
And i'm sure the anyone who bullies
fat people know someone who is fat

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:12.530
that they would never try to put down.
JOHN: Yeah

00:14:12.530 --> 00:14:14.340
I know what you mean.

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:16.340
I used to have braces, remember?

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:17.780
BENE: Oh yeah, when I first met you.

00:14:17.780 --> 00:14:21.450
JOHN: Yeah, remember Donny O'Malley who used to go on 
my bus?

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:23.300
He used to brutalize me.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.610
And call me things like train tracks until i just

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:27.940
decided not to take the bus anymore.

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:28.720
Well, I mean,

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:30.130
I know what you mean.

00:14:30.130 --> 00:14:33.300
And I know how ridiculous it is.

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:35.870
BENE: So you were picked on a lot?

00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:36.509
JOHN: Yeah.

00:14:36.509 --> 00:14:40.550
One day I just yelled at them and scared
the heck out of 'em.

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:45.790
Maybe that's what you should do, just freak out Arthur
and Megan get a megaphone and a soap box and start a riot

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:49.690
against weight prejudice.
BENE: I don't think starting a riot would do anything.

00:14:49.690 --> 00:14:51.930
It would get me a detention and 

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:53.800
probably a court date.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:56.430
But it wouldn't really teach Arthur and Megan
anything.

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:59.270
I'm just saying, don't knock fat people.

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:03.889
Cause it's just terribly mean and nasty and
viscous and lousy and

00:15:03.889 --> 00:15:05.140
rotten.
JOHN: Fact?
BENE: Fact.

00:15:07.930 --> 00:15:14.930
So, Class, I'd like to conclude my project with what I've learned
in the past month or so.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:20.690
Doctors seem to have their ducks in a row about 
this whole diet and exercise thing.

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:22.860
I eat well and I exercise.

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:26.260
I'm doing the best I can with the body I
have.

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:28.140
Everyone's body is different.

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:30.290
The science behind it is complicated.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:34.460
I can eat the same thing as any of you and our
bodies will respond differently.

00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:37.829
JOHN: I'm worried some of you might be putting gravy on 
your ice cream.

00:15:37.829 --> 00:15:40.430
BENE: I was looking for that!

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:43.650
Keeping friends around when you've got the blues
helps a lot.

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:47.160
There were some days when Arthur was on a
roll that just got to me.

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:52.770
I'm glad I have John and Erica around.
MOM: Hey, Bene, walk the dog!
BENE: I did already!

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:54.840
And my family. 

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:58.670
They're good to talk to because they listen and they
don't call me fatty or tubs like

00:15:58.670 --> 00:16:01.190
some people at school.

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:03.540
It does hurt to be teased.

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:07.000
But what sort of person does that?

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:10.130
They don't know how much it really affects me.

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:13.070
It's at least enough for me to spend a
whole lot more time that I needed to on

00:16:13.070 --> 00:16:16.590
this project.

00:16:16.590 --> 00:16:18.060
Well, that's it I think.

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:20.950
I've cleared up some myths about overweight
people,

00:16:20.950 --> 00:16:24.779
i've secretly recorded a classroom.
and I've said some

00:16:24.779 --> 00:16:26.580
not so nice things

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:27.550
about the teacher grading this.

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:30.580
Whoops!

